
## Part 1

1. Have you ever been part of a sport team?
  - Yes, I have - although it wasn’t **exactly by choice**. **Back in high school**, I **ended up** on the **class soccer team** for a **girls’ tournament** because we simply didn’t have enough female students. There were only about **five girls in my class**, so I kind of got **roped into it**. I wasn’t particularly talented, but it was still a valuable experience because it taught me how to **coordinate with teammates** and not give up even when I **felt out of my depth**.
2. Are team sport popular in your country?
  - Definitely. Team sports are **hugely popular** in my country, especially **football** - it has the biggest **fan base** by far. Besides that, people also **play and follow** **volleyball, badminton, and basketball**, depending on the region. **What I’ve noticed** is that team sports are popular partly because they’re **social** and **easy to organise** - you can just gather a group of friends after school or after work and start playing.
3. Do you like watching team games? Why?
  - It depends, but in general, yes - especially when the stakes are high. If it’s a **national or international match**, I genuinely enjoy the **team spirit** and the atmosphere. **It can be really exciting** to watch athletes **push themselves to the limit** and **represent their country** - sometimes the game becomes a real **nail-biter**.
    That said, I don’t usually watch casual or low-level matches because, honestly, it can feel a bit **time-consuming**, and I’d rather just watch highlights.
4. What are the differences between team sports and individual sports?
  - The biggest difference is **responsibility**. In team sports, success **depends on** **communication, cooperation, and team chemistry**, because you rely on each other and you have to **play for a shared goal**.  **In contrast**, individual sports are **more about** **self-discipline, consistency, and mental toughness**, because if you win or lose, it’s completely **on you** - you can’t **hide behind teammates**. So I’d say team sports build collaboration, while individual sports develop independence.

